<document class="article" options="12pt">
  <usepackage name="amsmath, amssymb, amsthm"/>
  <usepackage name="hyperref"/>
  <usepackage name="geometry" options="margin=1in"/>
  <usepackage name="microtype"/>

  <!-- Theorem environments -->
  <newtheorem name="theorem" title="Theorem"/>
  <newtheorem name="corollary" title="Corollary" shared="theorem"/>
  <newtheorem name="lemma"     title="Lemma"     shared="theorem"/>
  <theoremstyle style="remark"/>
  <newtheorem* name="remark" title="Remark"/>

  <body>
    <title>
      A Double-Sum Identity Connecting Geometric Series\\
      and the Riemann Zeta Function
    </title>
    <author>Nifemi Bosun</author>
    <date/>
    <maketitle/>

    <abstract>
      We prove the identity
      <dmath>
        \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr) = 1,
      </dmath>
      where <m>\zeta</m> denotes the Riemann zeta function. The proof proceeds
      by re-expressing each summand as a tail of the zeta series, interchanging
      the order of summation, evaluating the resulting geometric series in
      closed form, and recognising the outcome as a telescoping sum. Several
      consequences and related observations are noted.
    </abstract>

    <section name="Introduction">

      <p>
        The Riemann zeta function, defined for real <m>s > 1</m> by
        <dmath>
          \zeta(s) \;=\; \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^s},
        </dmath>
        occupies a central position in analytic number theory. Its values at
        positive integer arguments have been studied extensively; for instance,
        Euler's celebrated result <m>\zeta(2) = \pi^2/6</m> belongs to this
        family. Less commonly noted are identities involving <em>sums</em>
        taken over the integer arguments themselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        Observe that for each integer <m>k \geq 2</m>,
        <dmath>
          \zeta(k) - 1 \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k},
        </dmath>
        since the <m>n = 1</m> term of the zeta series equals <m>1</m>. The
        quantity <m>\zeta(k) - 1</m> therefore measures how much <m>\zeta(k)</m>
        exceeds its dominant term, and it decreases rapidly to zero as
        <m>k \to \infty</m>. It is natural to ask whether the series
        <m>\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}(\zeta(k)-1)</m> converges, and if so, to what
        value.
      </p>

      <p>
        The answer is both affirmative and striking: the sum equals exactly
        <m>1</m>. We prove this below.
      </p>

    </section>

    <section name="Main Result">

      <theorem>
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr) \;=\; 1.
        </dmath>
      </theorem>

      <proof>
        Substituting the series definition of <m>\zeta(k) - 1</m>, we write
        <eq id="eq:double">
          \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr)
          \;=\; \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k}.
        </eq>
        Since every term <m>1/n^k</m> is strictly positive, the double sum
        <eqref id="eq:double"/> is a sum of non-negative terms. By Tonelli's
        theorem (or, equivalently, by the classical rearrangement theorem for
        series of non-negative reals), the order of summation may be freely
        interchanged:
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k}
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k}.
        </dmath>
        For each fixed <m>n \geq 2</m>, the inner sum is a geometric series
        with first term <m>1/n^2</m> and common ratio <m>1/n \in (0, 1)</m>:
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k}
          \;=\; \frac{1/n^2}{1 - 1/n}
          \;=\; \frac{1}{n^2} \cdot \frac{n}{n-1}
          \;=\; \frac{1}{n(n-1)}.
        </dmath>
        Partial fractions give
        <m>\dfrac{1}{n(n-1)} = \dfrac{1}{n-1} - \dfrac{1}{n}</m>, so
        <dmath>
          \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n-1)}
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{n-1} - \frac{1}{n}\right).
        </dmath>
        This is a telescoping series whose partial sums satisfy
        <dmath>
          \sum_{n=2}^{N} \left(\frac{1}{n-1} - \frac{1}{n}\right)
          \;=\; 1 - \frac{1}{N} \;\longrightarrow\; 1
          \quad \text{as } N \to \infty.
        </dmath>
        Therefore <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}(\zeta(k)-1) = 1</m>.
      </proof>

    </section>

    <section name="Remarks and Consequences">

      <remark>
        The interchange of summation is the pivotal step. Because all summands
        are positive, no absolute convergence argument beyond positivity is
        required. Had the terms been of mixed sign, a more delicate
        justification (e.g.\ via dominated convergence or absolute summability)
        would be necessary.
      </remark>

      <remark>
        The identity can be seen as a <em>repackaging</em> of the well-known
        partial-fraction decomposition
        <dmath>
          \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n-1)} = 1.
        </dmath>
        What the zeta function contributes is the exact route from
        integer-valued Dirichlet series to that decomposition via geometric
        series. In this sense, the proof traces a path: <em>zeta values
        <m>\to</m> geometric series <m>\to</m> partial fractions <m>\to</m>
        telescoping</em>.
      </remark>

      <corollary>
        For any integer <m>m \geq 2</m>,
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=m}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr)
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^{m-1}(n-1)}.
        </dmath>
      </corollary>

      <proof>
        The same interchange of summation gives
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=m}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr)
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \sum_{k=m}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^k}
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1/n^m}{1 - 1/n}
          \;=\; \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^{m-1}(n-1)}.
        </dmath>
        The theorem corresponds to <m>m = 2</m>, in which case the
        right-hand side reduces to
        <m>\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n-1)} = 1</m>.
      </proof>

      <remark>
        The corollary shows that partial sums of our series (summing from
        <m>k = m</m> onward) admit closed-form expressions as Dirichlet-type
        series in <m>n</m>. As <m>m</m> grows, these tails converge more
        rapidly, consistent with <m>\zeta(k) - 1</m> being exponentially small
        for large <m>k</m>.
      </remark>

      <remark>
        A formal kinship exists with the identity
        <dmath>
          \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \bigl(\zeta(k) - 1\bigr)(-1)^k
          = \frac{1}{2} - \gamma,
        </dmath>
        where <m>\gamma</m> is the Euler<ndash/>Mascheroni constant, which can
        be established by analogous means using the alternating-sign version of
        the series swap. This suggests the present identity sits within a
        broader family parametrised by the sign pattern of
        <m>\zeta(k) - 1</m>.
      </remark>

    </section>

  </body>
</document>
